          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The responses treat “seed” as context-dependent, with two principal senses: God’s word or gospel sown in hearers, and offspring or promised lineage culminating in Christ. They also recognize children of the kingdom, kingdom growth, resurrection, faith, and sowing-and-reaping applications.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book unifies these usages under a generative principle: a planted word, life, or person reproduces after its kind. It includes God’s word, children, Messiah, and the growth of one planted person into a corporate house, people, or nation-tree.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Seed signifies a generative message or offspring—especially God’s word and the promised messianic lineage—which produces life, descendants, growth, or fruit according to context.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Seed is a planted generative word or life that reproduces after its kind, including a person capable of developing into a larger corporate people or tree.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains the consensus’s central word and offspring/Messiah cases but turns them into instances of a broader generative ontology. It additionally emphasizes a planted individual developing corporately into a house, people, or nation-tree, a boundary not explicitly formulated by the consensus responses.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction: the book affirms both dominant consensus identifications. The difference is that it subsumes them under a wider reproductive principle and extends that principle from individual seed to corporate historical growth.\",\"rationale\":\"The central referents recognized by consensus—God’s word, offspring or children, Christ, and kingdom growth—are all preserved. The relationship is therefore not divergent or novel. It is refined because the book materially broadens the extension by treating these senses as manifestations of one rule, “life planted to reproduce after its kind,” and by applying that rule to person-to-people or nation-tree development.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts clearly support reproduction after kind, the sown word, children as seed, Christ as promised seed, and seed growing into a tree. However, the single unified metaphysical rule and especially the language of one person growing into a “house” or “nation-tree” are synthetic inferences rather than explicit definitions in any one cited passage.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
